VERY SHORT XV6'S INTRODUCTION

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix
Version 6 (v6).  xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6,
but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.


WHAT WE DID

In this project we have extended xv6 adding four systems calls to
implement semaphores and also we made a new program: "pingpong". 
This new program simulate a ping-pong game 
(this sounds so cool, but just prints ping and pong interspersing N times)


BUILDING AND RUNNING OUR NEW VERSION OF XV6 ('$' says, it is a command to execute in the terminal)

- Clone this repository

- $ sudo apt-get install qemu-system-riscv64 gcc-riscv64-linux-gnu

- Go to repository directory path

- $ make qemu

- enjoy it (command "ls" lists all the user programs, including pingpong ;) )

- <CRTL-a> x (to quit qemu)


RUNNING TEST

To run the test we have to do (in terminal, no in xv6):

- $ touch .gdbinit.tmpl-riscv

- $ make grade


SOME DESIGN DECISIONS

- The syscalls are implemented in sysproc.c

- The semaphore structure is in proc.h

- The function to initialize semaphores and the array of semaphores are in proc.c

- The maximum number of semaphores is in param.h

All this design decisions trying to follow the structure and style of xv6 (we have tried our best :) )



